75, 0.0200347900390625, 0.003925323486328125, -0.043212890625, -0.0386962890625, 0.002429962158203125, -0.018707275390625, -0.01517486572265625, 0.04547119140625, 0.04571533203125, 0.0246124267578125, 0.019866943359375, 0.00952911376953125, 0.038330078125, 0.0193634033203125, -0.04840087890625, -0.004245758056640625, 0.0023403167724609375, 0.0302276611328125, 0.0012369155883789062, -0.0148773193359375, 0.01360321044921875, -0.00901031494140625, -0.0300750732421875, 0.0211944580078125, 0.0008192062377929688, 0.00017535686492919922, -0.0117340087890625, -0.0265350341796875, -0.002246856689453125, -0.00939178466796875, -0.013916015625, -0.0271759033203125, 0.01137542724609375, -0.01493072509765625, 0.0312042236328125, 0.007701873779296875, -0.0240478515625, -0.011260986328125, 0.007740020751953125, 0.01385498046875, -0.00485992431640625, 0.0153656005859375, 0.0099334716796875, -0.022308349609375, 0.016265869140625, -0.0167236328125, 0.00577545166015625, 0.0171356201171875, -0.02020263671875, -0.005222320556640625, 0.0234222412109375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent research has begun to investigate the use of Geometric Algebra (GA) in the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, most notably in the study of electrical circuits (see, e.g., [1], [2], [3]). *Geometric Algebra* is a mathematical framework built on a real vector space of dimension \\( n = p + q \\), with signature \\((p, q)\\) describing the numbers of basis vectors with positive (\\(p\\)) and negative (\\(q\\)) squares. Each geometric algebra \\( \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\) has \\( 2^n \\) basis blades formed by exterior products of the orthonormal basis vectors \\( \\{e_1, ..., e_n\\} \\), where each blade is of a certain *grade*, meaning it is the exterior product of exactly \\( r \\) basis vectors (grade-0: scalars; grade-1: vectors; grade-2: bivectors; etc.). The unique decomposition \\( M = \\sum_{r=0}^n \\langle M \\rangle_r \\) projects any multivector \\( M \\) onto its grade-\\( r \\) components. This versatility allows GA to encapsulate complex physical quantities as mixed-grade multivectors, rather than as mere scalars or vectors.\n\nTransforming the ordinary differential equations (ODEs) that describe system behavior from the time domain to the Laplace domain is foundational to systems analysis and design, enabling powerful tools for stability, frequency response, and solution analysis. Yet the extension of Laplace transforms to fully general geometric algebra signals remains unresolved.\n\n**Motivation and Identified Research Gap:**  \nExisting Laplace transforms are well understood for real- and complex-valued functions, and some results address special hypercomplex algebras such as quaternions [4] and split-complex numbers [5]. However, these approaches are restricted:  \n- They only handle scalar or specific algebra-valued functions, not arbitrary mixed-grade multivectors.  \n- They require commutative multiplication (e.g., [5]) or only partially address noncommutative cases (e.g., [4]).  \n- They are limited in dimension and algebraic generality.\n\n**Central Research Gap:** No prior framework rigorously defines the Laplace transform for *arbitrary* multivector-valued functions\u2014allowing general mixtures of grades and accommodating noncommutative multiplication\u2014for broad classes of geometric algebras. This prevents direct Laplace-domain analysis of, for example, time-dependent quantities combining vectors (current), bivectors (magnetic flux), and higher grades in a unified system description.\n\n**Objectives:**  \n1. Precisely define the Laplace transform for arbitrary multivector-valued functions in general geometric algebras \\(\\mathcal{G}_{p,q}\\), for signatures up to 5.\n2. Prove essential properties: linearity, differentiation, invertibility\u2014all respecting the grading and noncommutativity inherent to GA.\n3. Demonstrate the use of this framework in physical applications, enabling direct Laplace-domain analysis where previous tools fail.\n\n**Laplace Transform for Multivector-Valued Functions:**  \nLet \\( M(t): \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\) be a multivector-valued function, decomposed as \\( M(t) = \\sum_{r=0}^n \\langle M(t) \\rangle_r \\), where \\( \\langle M(t) \\rangle_r \\) denotes the grade-\\( r \\) component. We define the Laplace transform by  \n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}\\{M\\}(s) := \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} M(t)\\,dt = \\sum_{r=0}^n \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} \\langle M(t) \\rangle_r\\,dt\n\\]\n**Integration is performed componentwise:** that is, the Laplace transform of each grade-\\( r \\) part is computed separately as a standard scalar-valued Laplace integral, and the results are kept within their respective grades. Since \\( s \\in \\mathbb{C} \\) is scalar and \\( e^{-st} \\) is a real- or complex-valued scalar function, multiplication \\( e^{-st} M(t) \\) is scalar-multivector multiplication (not a geometric product). This ensures that, while the algebra\u2019s internal products may be noncommutative, the integral itself does not introduce new noncommutativity.\n\nChallenges addressed:  \n- Ensuring the treatment does not lose mixed-grade structure.\n- Verifying properties like the derivative rule (\\( \\mathcal{L}\\{\\frac{dM}{dt}\\}(s) = s \\mathcal{L}\\{M\\}(s) - M(0) \\)) hold even under noncommutative products (see below for explicit illustration).\n- Establishing that the transform is invertible whenever the scalar-valued Laplace transform is invertible on each grade.\n\n**Main Formal Results:**  \n*Theorem (Linearity and Differentiation):*  \nIf \\( M(t) \\) and \\( N(t) \\) are absolutely integrable \\(\\mathcal{G}_{p,q}\\)-valued functions and \\( a, b \\in \\mathbb{R} \\), then\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}\\{a M(t) + b N(t)\\}(s) = a \\mathcal{L}\\{M(t)\\}(s) + b \\mathcal{L}\\{N(t)\\}(s)\n\\]\nand  \n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\frac{dM}{dt}(t)\\right\\}(s) = s \\mathcal{L}\\{M(t)\\}(s) - M(0)\n\\]\nwith everything computed grade-by-grade. The multiplication by \\(s\\) is scalar and commutes with all multivectors. Internal products (such as multiplication of components after transformation) follow the GA\u2019s noncommutative rules.\n\n**Noncommutativity Illustrated:**  \nLet \\( A, B \\in \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\) and consider \\( M(t) = A t, N(t) = B t \\) with (for illustration) \\( AB \\neq BA \\).  \n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\frac{d}{dt}(AM(t) + BN(t))\\right\\}(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{A M'(t) + B N'(t)\\}(s)\n\\]\n\\[\n= A \\mathcal{L}\\{M'(t)\\}(s) + B \\mathcal{L}\\{N'(t)\\}(s) = A(s \\mathcal{L}\\{M\\}(s) - M(0)) + B(s \\mathcal{L}\\{N\\}(s) - N(0))\n\\]\nBut \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) multiply their Laplace components using the geometric (potentially noncommutative) product; thus, \\( A \\mathcal{L}\\{M\\}(s) + B \\mathcal{L}\\{N\\}(s) \\neq (A+B) \\mathcal{L}\\{M+N\\}(s) \\) in general, illustrating how the algebra\u2019s noncommutativity is retained in Laplace-domain expressions.\n\n**Explicit Example 1 \u2013 RLC Circuit (Mixed Grades):**  \nConsider an RLC circuit with current modeled by \\( I(t) = I_0(t) e_1 + \\Phi(t) e_{23} \\), where \\( e_1 \\) is a grade-1 (vector) basis element (e.g., for current), and \\( e_{23} \\) a grade-2 (bivector) (e.g., for magnetic flux) in \\(\\mathcal{G}_{3,0}\\).\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}\\{I(t)\\}(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{I_0(t)\\}(s) e_1 + \\mathcal{L}\\{\\Phi(t)\\}(s) e_{23}\n\\]\nThis permits analysis and manipulation of the combined vector and bivector components directly in the Laplace domain.\n\n**Explicit Example 2 \u2013 Spacetime Electrodynamics (Higher Grades and Noncommuting Terms):**  \nIn spacetime algebra (\\(\\mathcal{G}_{1,3}\\)), the electromagnetic field is often written as \\( F(t) = \\mathbf{E}(t) + I \\mathbf{B}(t) \\), where \\( \\mathbf{E}(t) \\) is the electric field (vector, grade-1), \\( \\mathbf{B}(t) \\) is the magnetic field (vector), and \\( I = e_0 e_1 e_2 e_3 \\) is the pseudoscalar (grade-4). The term \\( I\\mathbf{B}(t) \\) is a bivector, and \\( \\mathbf{E}(t) \\) and \\( I\\mathbf{B}(t) \\) generally do not commute.\nApplying the Laplace transform:\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}\\{F(t)\\}(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{\\mathbf{E}(t)\\}(s) + I \\mathcal{L}\\{\\mathbf{B}(t)\\}(s)\n\\]\nSuppose \\(\\mathbf{E}(t)\\) and \\(\\mathbf{B}(t)\\) are independent vector functions. Their Laplace transforms remain in their respective grades, but multiplication by \\(I\\) (a grade-4 element) does not, in general, commute with other algebra elements. Combinations and manipulations in the Laplace domain"}}